  "pages": [
    {
      "title": "What are Mirror & Water Images?",
      "body": "<p>A <strong>mirror image</strong> (plane mirror) shows an object as it would appear reflected in a vertical mirror: <strong>left and right are swapped</strong> (lateral inversion), while top and bottom stay the same. A <strong>water image</strong> is a reflection in a horizontal surface (like still water): <strong>top and bottom are swapped</strong>, while left and right stay the same.</p><div class='figure fig-svg'><svg viewBox='0 0 360 160' width='360' height='160'><text x='60' y='25' text-anchor='middle' class='label-sm'>Object</text><text x='180' y='25' text-anchor='middle' fill='var(--accent)' class='label-sm'>Mirror image</text><text x='300' y='25' text-anchor='middle' fill='var(--green)' class='label-sm'>Water image</text><line x1='120' y1='40' x2='120' y2='150' stroke='var(--text-dim)' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/><line x1='240' y1='40' x2='240' y2='150' stroke='var(--text-dim)' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/><polygon points='40,60 80,60 60,110' fill='var(--accent)'/><g transform='translate(240,0) scale(-1,1)'><polygon points='0,60 40,60 20,110' fill='var(--accent)'/></g><g transform='translate(240,120) scale(1,-1)'><polygon points='0,-60 40,-60 20,-10' fill='var(--accent)'/></g></svg><div class='fig-caption'>Mirror flips left-right; water flips top-bottom.</div></div><div class='callout tip'><strong>Mental trick</strong>For a mirror, imagine swapping every left/right feature. For water, flip the object upside down. Letters like <strong>A, H, I, M, O, T, U, V, W, X, Y</strong> look unchanged in a vertical mirror (vertically symmetric). Digits <strong>0, 8</strong> are symmetric.</div>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Mirror Images of Letters & Numbers",
      "body": "<p>Most letters change when mirrored. Practice by writing them reversed. Symmetric letters stay the same; asymmetric ones become their 'backward' form.</p><div class='figure fig-svg'><svg viewBox='0 0 320 110' width='320' height='110'><text x='40' y='40' text-anchor='middle' class='label-sm'>Original</text><text x='160' y='40' text-anchor='middle' fill='var(--accent)' class='label-sm'>Mirror</text><text x='270' y='40' text-anchor='middle' fill='var(--green)' class='label-sm'>Same?</text><text x='40' y='85' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Georgia,serif' font-size='30' font-weight='bold' fill='var(--text)'>B</text><g transform='translate(320,0) scale(-1,1)'><text x='160' y='85' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Georgia,serif' font-size='30' font-weight='bold' fill='var(--accent)'>B</text></g><text x='270' y='85' text-anchor='middle' fill='var(--red)' class='label'>No</text></svg><div class='fig-caption'>B is asymmetric — its mirror looks different.</div></div><div class='callout warn'><strong>Common trap</strong>In 'mirror image of a word' questions, each letter is mirrored individually and the letter order is reversed. Always check the options carefully — symmetric letters (A, H, I, M, O, T, U, V, W, X, Y) stay the same, asymmetric ones flip.</div>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Water Images & Clock Mirrors",
      "body": "<p>For water images, flip top and bottom. Letters horizontally symmetric about their middle (like <strong>B, C, D, E, H, I, K, O, X</strong>) look the same in water.</p><h3>Mirror clock trick</h3><p>If a clock shows a time in a mirror, the real time = <strong>11:60 − shown time</strong> (subtract hours and minutes separately, borrowing from the hour when minutes go negative).</p><div class='worked-example'><div class='we-q'>A clock in a mirror shows 4:10. What is the real time?</div><div class='we-sol'>11:60 − 4:10 = 7:50. Real time is 7:50.</div></div><div class='worked-example'><div class='we-q'>Real time is 3:00. What does the mirror show?</div><div class='we-sol'>11:60 − 3:00 = 8:60. Since 60 minutes = 1 hour, this is 9:00. Mirror shows 9:00.</div></div><div class='callout tip'><strong>Quick formula</strong>Mirror time + Real time = 11:60. Use this and you'll never go wrong on clock-mirror questions.</div>"
    }
  ],
